/*
 * The privacy notice, as drafted on 30 October 2026 for CRASA's legal
 * review (item 1h). Each line is "Heading. Body" and becomes a numbered
 * section on the legal page; a line that is only a heading opens a group.
 * Text in square brackets is a drafting note for CRASA: it is listed in the
 * back office as an outstanding item and never shown to readers.
 *
 * Retention periods state what the code defines: database backups 14 days
 * (docs/BACKUP_ACTION.md), the folders' recycle bin 30 days
 * (Document::BIN_DAYS), a signed-in session two hours without activity
 * (SESSION_LIFETIME), the cookie choice 12 months (public/js/consent.js).
 * Everything else is CRASA's decision and says so.
 *
 * Used by LegalPagesSeeder and by the migration that replaces the earlier
 * seeded text on an existing database.
 */

return [
    'en' => implode("\n\n", [
        'What this notice covers. This notice explains how CRASA handles personal data on this platform: the public website, the member area and the administration back office. It does not cover other websites CRASA links to, including the social networks where CRASA posts.',
        "Who is responsible. The Communications Regulators' Association of Southern Africa (CRASA), Plot 143, Unit 2, Gaborone International Finance Park, P.O. Box 25793, Gaborone, Botswana. Email crasa@crasa.org, telephone +267 315 84 68. [CRASA to confirm the data controller of record, and whether a data protection officer is appointed and how to reach them.]",
        'What the platform collects, and why.',
        'Accounts. Accounts for the member area and the back office are created by the Secretariat; there is no self-registration. An account holds a name, an email address, a role, the member state, authority or committee it belongs to, and a language preference. Passwords are stored only as a one-way hash and nobody, CRASA included, can read them. The account exists so that the people CRASA has authorised can sign in and see what their role allows.',
        'Enquiries. The contact form records your name, email address, subject and message, and your organisation and country if you give them, so that the Secretariat can reply to you.',
        "Delegate registrations. When CRASA opens registration for a meeting, the platform records the delegate's name, email address, role, authority and member state, and the arrival and departure details given, so that the Secretariat can organise the meeting.",
        'Data returns. Regulators submit indicator figures through the member area. The figures describe member states, not people. The platform records which account submitted, returned or validated each return and when, because a published regional figure must be traceable to its source.',
        'Server logs and the audit log. The web server records the address (IP) of each request, the time, the page requested and the browser, to keep the platform secure and to find faults. Actions taken in the member area and the back office are written to an audit log with the account and the time.',
        'Cookies and similar storage. Essential, always on: a session cookie that keeps a signed-in reader signed in, a security token that protects every form, and your language, accessibility settings and cookie choice, which are kept in your own browser. When the security check on public forms is switched on, Cloudflare Turnstile uses what it needs to tell people from automated traffic. Optional, off until you choose: posts from X, LinkedIn and Facebook, and the map tiles from OpenStreetMap. They load only after you accept them, or when you press "Show" on one item; they then send your IP address to that service, which may set its own cookies. There is no advertising cookie and no tracker on this platform. You can change your choice at any time with "Cookie settings" at the bottom of every page.',
        'The basis for using it. Account, data return and audit records are used because CRASA needs them to run the platform for its members. Enquiries and delegate registrations are used to answer the request you made. Server logs and essential cookies are needed to keep the platform secure and working. Optional content is loaded only with your consent, which you can withdraw. [CRASA and its counsel to confirm the legal basis for each use under the law that applies to CRASA.]',
        'How long it is kept. Database backups are kept for 14 days, then deleted. A file deleted from a member area folder stays in the recycle bin for 30 days, then is removed. A signed-in session ends after two hours without activity. Your cookie choice is kept for 12 months, then you are asked again. Enquiries, delegate registrations, accounts, server logs and the audit log: to be confirmed by CRASA. [CRASA to set retention periods for enquiries, delegate registrations, accounts, server logs and the audit log.]',
        'Who receives it. The hosting provider stores the platform, its database and its backups. Cloudflare stands in front of the website to protect it and pass each request on, so it handles your IP address and the page you asked for; it also counts page visits for CRASA without cookies. Brevo sends the platform\'s email in production (account invitations, password resets, confirmations), so it receives the recipient\'s address and the message. X, LinkedIn, Facebook and OpenStreetMap receive your IP address only if you choose to show their content. CRASA does not sell or rent personal data and does not share it for marketing. Inside the platform, what each account can see depends on its role and member state, checked on the server for every request. [CRASA to confirm the hosting provider and hosting location, and whether any data leaves the SADC region, including backups.] [CRASA to confirm whether any data is shared with COMESA, with SADC, or with a funder, and on what basis.]',
        'Your rights. You can ask what personal data the platform holds about you, ask for it to be corrected or deleted, and withdraw your consent to optional content at any time. Write to crasa@crasa.org. [CRASA and its counsel to state the rights available and the law they arise under, and the time within which CRASA answers a request.]',
        'Security. Transport is encrypted. Passwords are hashed. Access is checked on the server for every request rather than by hiding controls. Uploaded files cannot run code.',
        'Complaints. If you are not satisfied with CRASA\'s answer, the authority you can complain to is to be confirmed by CRASA. [CRASA to name the supervisory authority a reader may complain to, and the governing jurisdiction.]',
        'Changes. The date this notice was last updated is shown on this page. [CRASA to state how changes to this notice will be communicated.]',
    ]),

